id: k8s.hpa
kind: kubernetes
title: Horizontal Pod Autoscaler
aliases: hpa, autoscaling
tags: scaling, availability
questions:
  - how does autoscaling decide to add replicas?
  - how do you scale on backlog instead of CPU?
invariant: The HPA adjusts replica count toward a target metric ratio, bounded by min/max and by cooldowns.
answer_30s: HPA polls a metric (usually CPU, or queue backlog through an external adapter) and scales replicas so the average hits the target ratio, clamped to minReplicas/maxReplicas.
answer_2m: |
  The controller computes desired = ceil(current * currentMetric / targetMetric),
  with scale-up and scale-down stabilization windows to avoid flapping; it
  writes to the workload's replica count, so the Deployment controller does the
  actual work. Three prerequisites people miss: metrics-server (or an external
  metrics adapter) must exist, every Pod must declare CPU requests for a
  percentage target to mean anything, and scaling out only helps if the
  bottleneck is per-replica CPU -- not the database, the queue, or a mutex.
  Cluster capacity is the other limit: an HPA that wants 40 replicas on a
  cluster with room for 20 just queues Pending Pods.
when_to_use:
  - stateless workloads with measurable per-replica saturation
  - queue growth or backlog detected by an external metrics adapter
avoid_when:
  - stateful or single-writer bottlenecks; work that must be queued (scale the consumers, not the producers)
commands:
  - kubectl get hpa => current vs target metric and the current replica count
  - kubectl describe hpa api => the exact scaling events and why a decision was made
failure_modes:
  - no resource requests, so the percentage target is meaningless
  - maxReplicas set to the cluster limit, so scaling out is throttled by pending Pods
  - scaling on CPU while the real bottleneck is a shared database
follow_ups:
  - how would you autoscale on queue depth instead of CPU?
related:
  - k8s.deployment
  - k8s.resources.requests
  - design.backpressure
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/
